## Introduction
Most chloroplast proteins are encoded by the nuclear genome, synthesized in the cytosol as precursor proteins containing specific cleavable terminal presequences (chloroplast transit peptides: cTPs), and post-translationally transported into chloroplasts, where the cTPs are removed. The estimated number of nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins is thought to be about 1,500–4,500 based on a genome-wide prediction of the presence of cTPs by using computer programs (Zybailov et al. 2008). More recently, a variety of experimental approaches have also become available for identifying the subcellular localization of proteins, for example, mass spectrometry, which is used in proteomic surveys of subcellular components in Arabidopsis, has yielded information of plastid locations for approximately 1,100 proteins (Heazlewood et al. 2007). However, the large-scale experimental identification of protein subcellular localization remains too expensive or unfeasible. Under such circumstances, to analyze minimum predicted chloroplast proteins using our limited resources, we have chosen 2,090 as the predicted number of chloroplast proteins according to a report by Richly and Leister (2004), who used a conservative prediction strategy that employed a combination of several prediction data sets. This number is now thought to be too low and some of the genes in the present study may not encode the plastid proteins. However, it is a useful starting point for the large-scale analysis of nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins using tagged lines.
In 2010, we released the first version of the Chloroplast Function Database, which is a publicly available web-based resource that provides access to information on tagged lines for nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins, including their phenotypes and genotypes. The database contained $>1,700$ phenotypic data sets of transposons or T-DNA-tagged lines, including for plants with or without a visible phenotype as young seedlings on agar plates. Collection of these mutant resources and homozygous tagged lines was undertaken as a part of the Multinational Coordinated Arabidopsis thaliana Functional Genomics Project, which developed collections of mutants and mutant lines for researchers to provide coverage for all of the genes in Arabidopsis (reviewed in Annual Report 2012 at http://www. arabidopsis.org/portals/masc/). For our project, we used a large number of transposon- or T-DNA-tagged lines that were constructed from the databases of RIKEN, the John Innes Centre, the University of Wisconsin, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, the Max Planck Institute, INRA and the Salk Institute. These seed stocks were donated to their originating seed stock centers, and homozygous insertion lines were made available through the Arabidopsis Biological Resource Center (ABRC) and RIKEN BRC for researchers. The collection of insertion homozygotes will provide a powerful tool to accelerate the functional analysis of nuclear-encoded chloroplast proteins in Arabidopsis. Access to such large numbers of insertion homozygotes enables the plant biology community to screen a variety of genome-wide mutants of chloroplast proteins for any non-identifiable phenotype that can be seen by the naked eye. For example, by screening a series of homozygous lines, several mutants defective in ${\mathsf{N A D}}({\mathsf{P}}){\mathsf{H}}$ dehydrogenase (NDH) activity, which are termed chlororespiratory reduction (crr) mutants, were identified directly by using pulse amplitude modulated (PAM) fluorometric analysis (Okuda et al. 2007, Shimizu et al. 2008, Peng et al. 2011). Another mutant, which contained two alleles of light-harvesting complex (LHC)- like protein 3 (lil3-1 and lil3-2) mutants, was identified by large-scale HPLC-based screening, which selected for pigment biosynthesis mutants (Tanaka et al. 2010).
Additionally, by observing 3-week-old young plants on agar plates during the collection of homozygous tagged lines, we identified 100 or more mutants on the basis of visible phenotypes. Some of these were named apg (albino or pale-green) mutants, and detailed functional analysis of these mutants was carried out. We previously reported eight apg mutants: APG1, which is a protein involved in the methylation of plastoquinone (Motohashi et al. 2003); APG2, which is a tatC homolog involved in chloroplast protein translocation across the thylakoid membrane (Motohashi et al. 2001); APG3, which is a translation-release-factor homolog (Motohashi et al. 2007); APG6, which is an Hsp101 heat-shock protein homolog (Myouga et al. 2006); APG8, which is an iron superoxide dismutase 2 gene (Myouga et al. 2008); APG10, which is a BBM II isomerase involved in histidine biosynthesis (Noutoshi et al. 2005); APG13, which is a bacterial peptidoglycan synthesis enzyme (MurE) (Garcia et al. 2008); and APG17, which is an
AtLrgB protein which functions against cell death (Yamaguchi et al. 2012). Moreover, proteome analysis of three apg mutants was recently carried out, and common and specific features of protein accumulation were defined by using quantitative proteomics (Motohashi et al. 2012).
The information in the database developed in this project will be widely available to a large number of researchers and will provide the basis for a variety of research projects that rely on large-scale information on chloroplast proteins. The newly constructed second version of the Chloroplast Function Database offers significant improvements in usability and provides additional important information to the plant biology community, enabling searches for visible phenotypes of genes of interest. The long-term impact of these enabling tools and technologies on agriculture is expected to be profound because they would provide fundamental knowledge for the construction of new plant varieties with superior agronomic traits.
## Results
Improvements in phenotype monitoring and collection of insertion homozygotes in the Chloroplast Function Database
## Transmission electron microscope observations of mutant plastids
The chloroplasts of Chl-defective mutants, such as albino and pale-green, are generally believed to occur with reduction in the thylakoid membrane. The thylakoid membrane contains many Chl pigments that play important roles in the absorption of light energy in photosynthesis. Thus, reducing the amount of thylakoid membranes will lead to a reduction in Chl pigment. In all of the apg mutants that were analyzed for the detailed functions of the mutated genes, the abnormalities in the structure of the thylakoid membrane and the reduction in Chl pigment were proportionality related. Similarly, investigations into the unique morphological characteristics of the plastids of Chl-defective mutants offer the possibility of new functional analytical approaches to reveal their gene functions. This trial provides powerful tools for analyzing genes that encode unknown proteins. To this end, we performed detailed structural observations of the plastids of many Chl-defective mutants by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Wild-type chloroplasts showed typical lamellar organization consisting of granal and intergranal thylakoids arranged mainly along a long plastid axis. We obtained electron microscope images of plastids of 48 apg mutant lines and classified them into 11 groups based on the morphological features of the plastid (Table 4). The schematic diagram, which simplifies the characteristic structures of 11 groups, and representative electron microscopic images are shown in Fig. 1. The classification was achieved by changing the quantity of the stacked thylakoid membrane and by changing the internal constituents, such as the accumulation of starch grains and plastoglobules. The predominant class in these classifications was a mutant in which the quantity of the stacked thylakoid membrane was decreasing. However, several mutants that showed vaguely pale-green phenotypes when viewed with the naked eye showed plastids that hardly changed the inside structure of a chloroplast compared with that of the wild type. Interestingly, we frequently could obtain a mutant that includes starch grains in the plastid. The function of the mutant gene belonging to this class tended to categorize the genes involved in sucrose or hormonal biosynthesis even if it is not all involved. This result suggests that mutants that exhibit abnormal partitioning between starch and sugar occur and that this partitioning is regulated by sucrose phosphate synthetase, which is associated with stress and nutrient deprivation (reviewed Smeekens et al. 2010). Plants with mutations in the genes related to carbon fixation tended to have plastids that accumulated plastoglobules (Table 4).
Moreover, mutants that contained plastids that formed structures similar to a prolamellar body were also observed (Fig. 2). When plants are grown in the dark, etioplasts are usually found in the cells. Etioplasts contain the prolamellar body, a characteristic structure that consists of unorganized membrane aggregations in the plastids. When we observed the plastid inner structure of the mutants with white cotyledons under light irradiation, we confirmed that the plastids contained structures similar to a prolamellar body. Among these, we determined that one of the mutant genes (No. 41: At4g32260) encoded the ATPase F0 complex subunit that is located on the stroma-thylakoid membrane of chloroplasts, which suggests that proper arrangement of the ATPase F0 complex is important for the development of stroma-thylakoids. In another three mutants containing the plastid that forms a plexus structure similar to a prolamellar body, we could not link the relationship between the predicted gene function and the characteristic inner ultrastructure of the plastid.
## New user-friendly tools for data mining and integration
We developed a publicly accessible web-based resource, the Chloroplast Function Database II, at the same URL as before (http://rarge.psc.riken.jp/chloroplast/). Release 2 was greatly improved compared with release 1, and it can be used to obtain detailed information on our project; to search using the Arabidopsis Genome Initiative (AGI) locus ID, mutant line number, protein function or phenotypic category; to obtain lists of genes and mutants that match a phenotypic category; and to obtain sequence data of PCR primer sets. Besides this, the improvements in release 2 are as follows: an increase in the number of isolated mutants with visible phenotypes accompanying the increase in the observed number of tagged lines; the addition of searchable data of the line number where we could not confirm the T-DNA insertion at the flanking sequence positions of the SIGnAL T-DNA Express database, which is called a wild type (WT); the addition of searchable data of the line number that we were able to obtain as homozygotes with no phenotype (HM); the addition of plastid images for apg mutants by TEM; and the addition of links between other reliable database across the AGI locus ID. Any line number for which we could not confirm the T-DNA insertion at the targeted position becomes useful when checking the existence of a T-DNA insertion in a gene of interest by designing new primers that allow the amplification of much longer than expected PCR products. The list of 1,740 homozygous mutants can be easily downloaded. These lines are also available from the various Arabidopsis stock centers (RIKEN, BRC and ABRC) for which we have donated the insertion homozygotes to their originating seed stock centers. In this database, the phenotype signifies one to one correspondence with all the lines and gene numbers (AGI), thus rendering it very easy to use. When the AGI locus ID is used as the search keyword, the results indicate the presence of any additional mutant alleles and their phenotypes.
Moreover, direct links between different databases across the AGI locus ID allow users to analyze gene functions in many different dimensions or angles, such as gene annotation (TAIR), co-expression data (ATTED-II), T-DNA insertion site (SIGnAL), RIKEN Ds insertion site (RARGE) and subcellular proteomic data (SUBA3) and the GFP database.
## Web interface
The Chloroplast Function Database II provides the opportunity to search and browse all experimental results without access restriction via the Internet using a Web browser. It has two search facilities: one is entering free keywords that accept a resource number, locus number and gene description, and the other is checking the boxes of phenotypes that were obtained in experiments (Fig. 3A). Users can also filter results to display only the lines that were obtained for TEM images by checking the box. When a search is performed using the locus number or gene description as a keyword, it shows the records of lines that are predicted to disrupt the corresponding locus. The search result includes records such as the resource number, locus number, gene description released from TAIR and phenotypes, and places them in a single table (Fig. 3B). Users can examine details by following the links from each resource number. The resource detail pages not only include the test results and the gene description of the disrupted locus, but also contain hyperlinks to related information, to the information on the mutant line that disrupted the same locus in the external database and to the information of mutant lines with the same phenotype in the external database (Fig. 3C, D). The Chloroplast Function Database is an ongoing project of RIKEN and will be updated as new data become available.
## Discussion
The relationships between genotypes and phenotypes are an important consideration for the development of a systematic approach to the functional analysis of Arabidopsis genes. Using the tagged line collections, we isolated 332 mutants representing 210 genes $19\%$ of the collected genes) that exhibited either seedling phenotypes or embryo-lethal phenotypes (Table 2). Eighty-three of the 210 genes had multiple mutant alleles that showed nearly the same phenotypes, which can be used to confirm the genes responsible for mutant phenotypes. The Chloroplast Function Database II provides a useful and comprehensive information resource to help researchers connect individual Arabidopsis genes to plastid functions based on phenotype analysis of tagged lines. It contains information on mutant phenotypes such as visible Chl deficiencies, altered morphologies and defective embryos, and thus provides a useful resource to help researchers select mutant lines for further analysis of their gene functions.
In T-DNA-tagged lines, we could not confirm the T-DNA insertions in the genes at high frequency as in a previous study (Myouga et al. 2010). These results were probably caused by inaccurate mapping of the insertion sites in the collections as a result of bulk sequencing of amplified T-DNA-flanking regions, in which multiple products were obtained and their start sites were not correctly sequenced. To solve these problems, users should either design new primers that will allow the amplification of fragments that are much longer than the PCR products obtained in this study or obtain the seeds of different mutant alleles. However, next-generation sequencing has enabled rapid and inexpensive indexing of insertion mutants, which has led to vast improvements in the accuracy of the information on T-DNA insertion positions. The application of such new technology has resulted in the recent progress in the collection of homozygous lines. In fact, among the 121 homozygous lines that were ordered from the SALK Homozygote T-DNA collection, we were unable to detect the T-DNA insertion at the reported position for only 18 lines $(15\%)$ . The frequency of lines without a T-DNA insertion decreased to about half compared with that before $(32\%)$ .
The upgraded database offers high resolution TEM images of plastid morphology of apg mutants and attempts to link to the ultrastructures of plastids of mutants with their gene functions. The trial partially succeeded in describing the clustering of plastid morphology into 11 classes, and we found a common functional trend in the characteristic features of the inner structure of the plastid. The collection of images of apg mutant plastids provides a useful resource that will help researchers analyze mutants that encode proteins of unknown function, although further studies are still needed. However, there are various problems in terms of the morphological observation of plastids by using an electron microscope. When two or more plastids exhibiting different internal structures exist within one cell of a mutant, duplicate classification will occur. Moreover, the locations of plant cell/ tissues are difficult to determine in leaves for which the plastid images are obtained using an electron microscope, thereby making it difficult to determine whether abnormal inner structures occur in whole cells of the leaves. The preparation of TEM samples, which are arranged in terms of size and form, for leaves of 3-week-old plants on agar plates is also difficult because the growth rates of mutants are different from each other. There were several mutants with plastids that showed very little difference in the stacked thylakoid membrane structure of the chloroplast compared with that of the wild type. However, this did not confirm the presence of normal chloroplasts in the cells of pale-green leaves. Even if inner chloroplast membrane structures were normal, the number of chloroplast might be altered. Although some difficult problems were encountered as mentioned above, no other database consists of such a large number of electron microscope images of the plastid of apg mutants. Thus, our database may serve as a useful tool for revealing the unknown structural plasticity of the plastid.
## Materials and Methods
## Plant materials and growth conditions
Information on the collections of tagged lines can be obtained from the RARGE (RIKEN Arabidopsis Genome Encyclopedia;
http://rarge.psc.riken.jp/) database for the RIKEN Ds-tagged lines and from the T-DNA express website (http://signal.salk.edu) for the other tagged lines. Seeds that were surface-sterilized with chlorine gas were plated on GM agar plates containing $1\%$ sucrose (Myouga et al. 2010). The plants were stored at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 3 d to improve germination rates and then grown in illuminated growth chambers (CF-405, TOMY-Seiko) with approximately $75\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ photon $\mathfrak{m}^{-2}\ \mathfrak{s}^{-1}$ at $22^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under a $16\mathsf{h}$ light $^{\prime}8\mathsf{h}$ dark cycle (long-day conditions) for 3 weeks.
## Selection of insertion homozygotes from SALK T-DNA-tagged lines
We sowed the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ seeds of the Salk T-DNA-tagged lines (Alonso et al. 2003) on GM plates without kanamycin because some of the lines did not show a drug-resistant phenotype on account of silencing of the marker gene. We directly genotyped at least 8–20 plants of the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ generation by PCR analysis and determined whether homozygous lines existed. If we could not obtain homozygous plants in the $\mathsf{F}_{1}$ generation, we then repeated genotyping for up to 20 plants of the $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ generation.
## Image recording
After suitable training, our experienced assistants systematically observed and recorded the visible seedling phenotype of each line that grew under constant conditions on the agar plates. We define ‘mutant’ phenotypes as plants that differed visibly from the wild-type plants; plants with no visible differences were defined as wild-type phenotypes. Digital images of the mutants were recorded using a digital camera (Olympus). High resolution digital images of the plastid of mutants were obtained by TEM as previously described (Motohashi et al. 2001).
